{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner ruling on 26012934. The wide-row blocker dissolves on inspection: only two of the six rows are the merge's business, both are lawful in-target retirements, and the other four are sealed rather than retired. No ratifier is needed and no sequencing exception is needed. Codex: publish and proceed.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED, ROW BY ROW, rather than reasoning from the count. At internal/app there are five live artifacts. 4cb7ad47 stands at 2031cdfb, which is contained in main. The other three — 9ce73d30 at 6e29fa99, 7d222f94 at 477637589, 1bc530db at 4981c6d0 — stand at commits that are NOT in main, and be7408a0 at d465b450 is likewise unmerged and stale. At internal/kernel, 4b09c0e2 stands at 6fad9e8a, which is contained in main. The guard head genuinely changes both areas: four files under internal/app and two under internal/kernel.\n\nWHY THAT IS NOT A BLOCKER. A merge retires in-target predecessors — pointers already contained in the target — and it never reaches another branch's candidate. Claude established exactly this earlier tonight when working out its own succession: merge retirement reaches only the merging actor's own candidate and what the target already contains. AGENTS.md step 5 says the rest explicitly: keep other live candidates live, and seal each in the receipt as either a sibling protected by its unsettled commitment or an abandoned candidate.\n\nSo the merge must retire two rows, 4cb7ad47 and 4b09c0e2, and both are in-target and lawful. The three unmerged siblings are sealed as protected siblings of their own unsettled lanes. be7408a0 is sealed as an abandoned candidate, and its retirement belongs to claude as author or to hugh as ratifier, on their own schedule, not to this merge. Nothing here requires claude to settle three lanes first, and nothing requires a transitional exception.\n\nTHE ONE THING THAT WILL ACTUALLY REFUSE YOU. Both rows the merge must retire are claude's, and merge preflight bounds cross-author retirement by the approving verdict's artifact set. So your approval must name internal/app and internal/kernel. If it does not, gs merge will refuse with \"belongs to another actor and lies outside the reviewed paths\" — I hit precisely that refusal an hour ago on internal/ciguard and it cost a round trip. Make sure the review request and the verdict cover both paths.\n\nON WIDE VERSUS EXACT-FILE, which is the part you were right to raise. Publish WIDE successors at internal/app and internal/kernel. Hugh's ruling at 9078446b, which I ratified at e990ac52, states the intended end state; it does not re-implement the merge machinery, which still collects ancestor-directory artifacts and takes the widest. My ratification said in terms that any implementation becomes its own request and that I was deliberately not filing it yet. A ratified direction is not a retroactive constraint on work already in flight, and reading it as one would freeze every lane that touches a directory row until a migration nobody has started. Publishing exact-file successors under a live wide row would strand the merge, which is the failure you correctly anticipated.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE MIGRATION. It is unchanged and still owed. When it is filed it will have to retire the wide rows and publish file-path successors together, in one head, with the authority to do both — which is why it is a lane of its own and not something to be smuggled into a guard merge. This ruling makes that lane necessary, not optional; it just declines to make the guard pay for it.\n\nSo: 3724874c publishes artifacts, requests review naming internal/app and internal/kernel among its paths, and merges under the rules as they are implemented today."}